\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces Two ways to read the wiggle, drawn from the same forecast spectrum (offsets exaggerated for visibility). A \emph  {dilation} slides the bump in separation without changing its shape: that is what the BAO parameters $\alpha _\perp $ and $\alpha _\parallel $ measure, and it is a geometry measurement --- where the feature sits. \emph  {Growth} raises the whole curve where it stands: that is what $f\sigma _8$ measures, an amplitude. The distinction governs this dissertation's results: smoothly distributed contamination power mimics a change of amplitude far more readily than a shift of a feature's position, and Chapter~\ref {sec:bao} finds the measured tolerance for $f\sigma _8$ to be ten to twenty-five times tighter than for the dilations.}}{5}{figure.1.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:intro:dilation}{{1.4}{5}{Two ways to read the wiggle, drawn from the same forecast spectrum (offsets exaggerated for visibility). A \emph {dilation} slides the bump in separation without changing its shape: that is what the BAO parameters $\alpha _\perp $ and $\alpha _\parallel $ measure, and it is a geometry measurement --- where the feature sits. \emph {Growth} raises the whole curve where it stands: that is what $f\sigma _8$ measures, an amplitude. The distinction governs this dissertation's results: smoothly distributed contamination power mimics a change of amplitude far more readily than a shift of a feature's position, and Chapter~\ref {sec:bao} finds the measured tolerance for $f\sigma _8$ to be ten to twenty-five times tighter than for the dilations}{figure.1.4}{}}
